---
import { initialStory, storyView, storyLessons, pizzaMapping, roleConnections } from '../lib/pizza-story';
const view=storyView(initialStory);
---
<details class="px" id="pizza-explainer" data-pizza-story>
  <summary class="px-summary"><span class="ag-eyebrow">OPTIONAL / AN EVERYDAY EXPLANATION</span><strong>Ports, adapters, and pizza.</strong><span class="px-summary-hint" data-pizza-hint>Open the three-step story ↓</span></summary>
  <div class="px-body">
    <p class="px-intro">A pizza shop can accept an order through a website or at the counter. Its pricing rules stay the same. Payment providers can change too. <strong>Hexagonal architecture gives software those boundaries.</strong></p>
    <div class="px-lessons" role="group" aria-label="Architecture story steps">{storyLessons.map((lesson,index)=><button type="button" data-pizza-lesson={index} aria-pressed={index===0}><span>0{index+1}</span>{lesson}</button>)}</div>
    <div class="px-mode" role="group" aria-label="Architecture example"><button type="button" data-pizza-mode="pizza" aria-pressed="true">Pizza shop</button><button type="button" data-pizza-mode="ai" aria-pressed="false">My AI system</button></div>
    <p class="px-status" aria-live="polite" data-pizza-change>{view.changed}</p>
    <div class="px-diagram" data-pizza-focus="incoming">
      <article class="px-adapter" data-pizza-part="incoming"><p class="ag-eyebrow">OUTSIDE / INCOMING ADAPTER</p><h3 data-pizza-input>{view.selectedInput}</h3><label for="pizza-channel">How does the request arrive?<select id="pizza-channel" data-pizza-channel><option value="first">{view.inputOptions[0]}</option><option value="second">{view.inputOptions[1]}</option></select></label><p class="px-small" data-pizza-payload>{view.inputPayload}</p><span class="px-contract"><small>APPLICATION ENTRY</small><strong data-pizza-entry>{view.incomingContract}</strong><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span></article>
      <article class="px-core" data-pizza-part="core"><svg viewBox="0 0 300 270" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="150,3 297,70 297,200 150,267 3,200 3,70"/></svg><div><p class="ag-eyebrow">INSIDE / THE CORE</p><h3 data-pizza-core>{view.coreTitle}</h3><ul data-pizza-rules>{view.coreLines.map(line=><li>{line}</li>)}</ul><span class="px-core-stable">Same core rules</span></div></article>
      <article class="px-adapter" data-pizza-part="outgoing"><p class="ag-eyebrow">OUTSIDE / OUTGOING ADAPTER</p><h3 data-pizza-provider>{view.selectedProvider}</h3><label for="pizza-provider">Which implementation connects?<select id="pizza-provider" data-pizza-provider-select><option value="first">{view.providerOptions[0]}</option><option value="second">{view.providerOptions[1]}</option></select></label><p class="px-small">A replaceable implementation behind an application-owned contract.</p><span class="px-contract"><small>OUTGOING PORT</small><strong data-pizza-port>{view.outgoingContract}</strong><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span></article>
    </div>
    <div class="px-explanation" aria-live="polite"><div><p class="ag-eyebrow">WHAT STAYS THE SAME?</p><p data-pizza-unchanged>{view.unchanged}</p></div><div><p class="ag-eyebrow">WHAT IS A PORT?</p><p data-pizza-contract>{view.contractMeaning}</p></div></div>
    <p class="px-small px-boundary">The arrows above show requests and contracts. Source-code dependencies point toward the core-owned contracts. A hexagon is a drawing convention; it does not prescribe six layers.</p>
    <section class="px-role-bridge" data-pizza-roles hidden aria-labelledby="pizza-roles-heading"><h3 id="pizza-roles-heading">How the four runtime roles fit</h3><p>Hexagonal architecture describes the boundaries. Telemetry, policy, scheduling and execution describe responsibilities across those boundaries.</p><div>{roleConnections.map(([name,explanation])=><article><h4>{name}</h4><p>{explanation}</p></article>)}</div><p class="px-small">The routing API currently composes the policy and scheduler directly. “Request a route” is the application entry being illustrated, not a claim of an extra use-case interface in the code.</p><a class="ag-link" href="#routing-lab">Explore these roles in the routing lab ↑</a></section>
    <details class="px-map"><summary>Show the pizza-to-Aegis mapping</summary><div class="px-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Pizza shop</th><th>Architecture concept</th><th>Aegis</th></tr></thead><tbody>{pizzaMapping.map(row=><tr>{row.map((cell,index)=>index===0?<th>{cell}</th>:<td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>
    <div class="px-footer"><p class="px-small" data-pizza-limit>{view.limitation}</p><button type="button" class="ag-link" data-pizza-reset>Reset the story ↺</button></div>
    <p class="px-credit">Teaching approach inspired by <a href="https://www.thoughtworks.com/insights/blog/architecture/hexagonal-architecture-explained-practical-example" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kinle Greshka’s Thoughtworks article ↗</a>. This diagram, wording and interactive illustration were created for this project.</p>
    <noscript><p class="px-small">The pizza example is readable without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to swap the adapters and reveal the AI mapping.</p><style>.px-lessons,.px-mode,.px-adapter label,[data-pizza-reset]{display:none}</style></noscript>
  </div>
</details>
<script>import { initPizzaStory } from '../scripts/pizza-story'; const root=document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-pizza-story]'); if(root)initPizzaStory(root);</script>
